<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_reindex.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-reindex">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-reindex">
  <primary>REINDEX VLABEL</primary>
 </indexterm>
 <indexterm zone="cypher-reindex">
  <primary>REINDEX ELABEL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>REINDEX VLABEL</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>REINDEX VLABEL</refname>
  <refpurpose>rebuild every index on a label</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
REINDEX [ ( <replaceable class="parameter">option</replaceable> [, ...] ) ] { VLABEL | ELABEL } <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable>

<phrase>where <replaceable class="parameter">option</replaceable> can be one of:</phrase>

    CONCURRENTLY [ <replaceable class="parameter">boolean</replaceable> ]
    TABLESPACE <replaceable class="parameter">new_tablespace</replaceable>
    VERBOSE [ <replaceable class="parameter">boolean</replaceable> ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-reindex-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>REINDEX VLABEL</command> rebuilds every index on a vertex label and
   <command>REINDEX ELABEL</command> every index on an edge label. That covers the
   label's own indexes, every property index on it, and the index on its TOAST relation.
  </para>

  <para>
   Rebuilding marks an invalid index valid again, which makes this statement the
   counterpart to <literal>DISABLE INDEX</literal>. There is no
   <literal>ENABLE INDEX</literal>. To load in bulk without maintaining the indexes, run
   <literal>ALTER VLABEL <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> DISABLE
   INDEX</literal>, then the load, then
   <literal>REINDEX VLABEL <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The label is looked up in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>. The name
   cannot carry the graph: <literal>REINDEX VLABEL rebuildme.movie</literal> is a syntax
   error. A label that is not there is reported by its qualified name, such as
   <literal>relation "rebuildme.nosuch" does not exist</literal>, because
   <varname>graph_path</varname> has already been applied to it.
  </para>

  <para>
   Each statement takes one kind of label. <command>REINDEX VLABEL</command> on an edge
   label fails with <literal>REINDEX VLABEL cannot REINDEX edge label</literal>, and
   <command>REINDEX ELABEL</command> on a vertex label with the matching message.
  </para>

  <para>
   Options go in parentheses before the label keyword.
   <literal>REINDEX VLABEL CONCURRENTLY movie</literal> is a syntax error here, though
   <command>REINDEX TABLE</command> accepts that bare keyword spelling. The older
   spelling <literal>REINDEX LABEL</literal> does not exist and is a syntax error at
   <literal>LABEL</literal>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-reindex-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Rebuild the indexes without locking out writes to the label, as
      <command>REINDEX</command> does for a table.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>TABLESPACE</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">new_tablespace</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Write the rebuilt indexes to the named tablespace instead of the one they are in
      now.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>VERBOSE</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Report each index as it is finished.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">boolean</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal> and <literal>VERBOSE</literal> each accept an
      explicit boolean, so <literal>(VERBOSE false)</literal> is the same as leaving
      <literal>VERBOSE</literal> out.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Label whose indexes are rebuilt, in the graph named by
      <varname>graph_path</varname>. It cannot be qualified with a graph name.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-reindex-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>. They
   build a graph of their own and drop it at the end:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH rebuildme;
SET graph_path = rebuildme;
CREATE VLABEL movie;
CREATE ELABEL directed;
CREATE PROPERTY INDEX movie_title_idx ON movie (title);
CREATE (:movie {title: 'Arrival'});
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Turning the indexes off leaves them in place but invalid, so a load does not maintain
   them. After <literal>ALTER VLABEL movie DISABLE INDEX</literal>,
   <literal>\d rebuildme.movie</literal> marks both the label's primary key
   <literal>movie_pkey</literal> and the property index
   <literal>movie_title_idx</literal> as <literal>INVALID</literal>. One statement builds
   them, and the same <literal>\d</literal> then shows neither marked:
<programlisting>
REINDEX VLABEL movie;

REINDEX
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>VERBOSE</literal> reports each index as it is finished. An edge label's three
   indexes are all rebuilt, and so is the index on the TOAST relation that holds
   oversized property maps. The TOAST index name carries the table's OID, so it differs
   from run to run:
<programlisting>
ALTER ELABEL directed DISABLE INDEX;
REINDEX (VERBOSE) ELABEL directed;

ALTER ELABEL
INFO:  index "pg_toast_NNNNN_index" was reindexed
DETAIL:  CPU: user: 0.00 s, system: 0.00 s, elapsed: 0.00 s
INFO:  index "directed_id_idx" was reindexed
DETAIL:  CPU: user: 0.00 s, system: 0.00 s, elapsed: 0.00 s
INFO:  index "directed_start_idx" was reindexed
DETAIL:  CPU: user: 0.00 s, system: 0.00 s, elapsed: 0.00 s
INFO:  index "directed_end_idx" was reindexed
DETAIL:  CPU: user: 0.00 s, system: 0.00 s, elapsed: 0.00 s
REINDEX
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Rebuild without locking writes out. <literal>(TABLESPACE pg_default)</literal> in the
   same position rebuilds into that tablespace:
<programlisting>
REINDEX (CONCURRENTLY) VLABEL movie;

REINDEX
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Each statement takes one kind of label:
<programlisting>
REINDEX VLABEL directed;

ERROR:  REINDEX VLABEL cannot REINDEX edge label
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
REINDEX ELABEL movie;

ERROR:  REINDEX ELABEL cannot REINDEX vertex label
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The name cannot carry the graph, and <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal> belongs inside
   the parentheses:
<programlisting>
REINDEX VLABEL rebuildme.movie;

ERROR:  syntax error at or near "."
LINE 1: REINDEX VLABEL rebuildme.movie;
                                ^
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
REINDEX VLABEL CONCURRENTLY movie;

ERROR:  syntax error at or near "CONCURRENTLY"
LINE 1: REINDEX VLABEL CONCURRENTLY movie;
                       ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>DROP GRAPH rebuildme CASCADE</literal> drops the graph the examples built.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-reindex-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>REINDEX TABLE</command> on a label's table does the same job and needs no
   setting to do it, so <literal>REINDEX TABLE rebuildme.movie</literal> also brings a
   disabled label's indexes back. Prefer the label spellings anyway. They check that the
   label is of the kind that was named, and they take a name resolved in
   <varname>graph_path</varname> rather than one qualified with a schema.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>REINDEX SCHEMA</command> on a graph's schema rebuilds every index in the
   graph, and <command>REINDEX INDEX</command> rebuilds one index by name. Both are the
   ordinary SQL statements, with no form that knows about labels.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-reindex-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no index DDL and nothing that rebuilds an index. Its catalog
   modifying statements reach only schemas, graphs and graph types, in clause 12, so
   these two spellings are an AgensGraph extension.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>REINDEX VLABEL</command> and <command>REINDEX ELABEL</command> are
   <command>REINDEX TABLE</command> with a target that knows about labels. The options
   are <command>REINDEX</command>'s own options and mean the same things. Two spelling
   differences matter. The target name is resolved in <varname>graph_path</varname> and
   cannot be qualified with a schema. And <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal> is accepted
   only as an option in parentheses, never as a bare keyword after the label keyword.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

